"@@@ The length of _items (internal array) should be greater than _size (number of actual elements) in the function."

This comment implies that before the function finishes executing, the length of the internal array (_items) should be greater than the number of elements it actually holds (_size). So, the assert statement will be:

```cs
Debug.Assert(_items.Length > _size)
```

However, it seems there is an issue in the current implementation. The assertion `Debug.Assert(_items.Length > _size)` will not hold true because the length of the array `_items` does not necessarily hold the actual number of elements in it after the operation. To correctly assert the size of the array, an `Add()` or `RemoveAt()` operation should be performed after the assert statement. 

Note: To check the length of the internal array at the beginning of the function, we need to make sure the length changes (at least) one place in the function. There might be other places elsewhere in the code where the capacity of the array is increased or decreased (`EnsureCapacity()`). If we cannot find other places that alter the size of internal array, then the current assert statement will not be appropriate. Therefore, it's critical to thoroughly analyze the implementation of the function to ensure the correct assert statement.--------------------
